# Solution — GIAM 7.2 Problem 6

## Problem

State necessary and sufficient conditions for the existence of an *Eulerian circuit* in a graph.

## Definitions

A (multi-)graph $ G = (V, E) $ has a vertex set $ V $ and an edge multiset $ E $, where each edge connects two vertices (parallel edges and self-loops allowed; a self-loop at $ v $ contributes $ 2 $ to $ \deg(v) $). An *Eulerian circuit* is a *closed walk* that traverses every edge of $ G $ exactly once. The graph is *connected* if there is a walk between any two vertices.

## Theorem (Euler, 1736)

**A finite (multi-)graph $ G $ with at least one edge has an Eulerian circuit if and only if both of the following hold:**

$$$
\begin{aligned}
\textbf{(C1)} & \quad \text{every vertex of $ G $ has \textit{even} degree, and}\\
\textbf{(C2)} & \quad \text{every edge of $ G $ lies in a single connected component}\\
              & \quad \text{(equivalently, $ G $ is connected after deleting isolated vertices).}
\end{aligned}
$$$

When $ G $ is itself connected (no isolated vertices to worry about), condition (C2) is automatic and the criterion collapses to:

> *A connected graph admits an Eulerian circuit iff every vertex has even degree.*

We prove both directions. Necessity rests on the same parity / handshake observation behind Problem $ 2 $; sufficiency is the constructive *Hierholzer's algorithm*.

## Proof of Necessity ($ \Rightarrow $)

Suppose $ G $ has an Eulerian circuit $ C $.

**(C1) — every vertex has even degree.** Fix any vertex $ v $. Every time $ C $ visits $ v $, it arrives along one edge and departs along a different edge (since $ C $ uses each edge only once, it cannot leave by the edge it arrived on). At the start/end of $ C $ — which is the same vertex $ v_0 $ by definition of "circuit" — the initial departure and final arrival also pair up. So the edges incident to $ v $ are partitioned into *pairs* (arrive, depart), forcing $ \deg(v) $ to be even.

**(C2) — all edges in one component.** A walk cannot "jump" between connected components: every consecutive pair of edges in $ C $ shares a vertex, so the edges of $ C $ all belong to a single component. Since $ C $ uses *every* edge of $ G $, all edges must lie in that one component. $ \square $

## Proof of Sufficiency ($ \Leftarrow $) — Hierholzer's Algorithm

Suppose (C1) and (C2) hold. We construct an Eulerian circuit by *Hierholzer's algorithm* (1873). Without loss of generality discard isolated vertices: assume $ G $ is connected with every vertex of positive, even degree.

**Step 1 — Greedy walk that always closes.** Pick any vertex $ v_0 $. Start a walk from $ v_0 $, traversing any unused edge; at each subsequent vertex, follow another unused edge as long as one exists.

*Claim.* The walk *cannot get stuck* at any vertex $ w \ne v_0 $.

*Proof of claim.* Suppose the walk has just arrived at $ w $ via some edge. Count edge-uses at $ w $: the walk has entered $ w $ some number of times and left some number of times, plus the final arrival. By the alternation "enter/leave/enter/leave/...", the walk has used an *odd* number of edges at $ w $ so far. Since $ \deg(w) $ is even (by C1), at least one edge at $ w $ remains unused — the walk can continue.

So the walk can only halt at $ v_0 $ itself. Since $ G $ has only finitely many edges, the walk halts in finitely many steps. Call this closed walk $ W_0 $; it uses some subset of the edges of $ G $.

**Step 2 — If $ W_0 $ covers $ E $, we are done.** Otherwise, let $ G' $ be the graph obtained by deleting the edges of $ W_0 $ from $ G $. Two facts:

- *$ G' $ still has all even degrees.* At each vertex $ v $, the walk $ W_0 $ used an even number of edges (two per visit, paired up — same argument as the necessity proof). So removing those edges from $ \deg_G(v) $ — still even — leaves $ \deg_{G'}(v) $ even.
- *There is a vertex of $ W_0 $ with a $ G' $-edge.* If not, then every vertex visited by $ W_0 $ has all of its $ G $-edges accounted for in $ W_0 $, so the edges of $ W_0 $ form their own connected component disjoint from the rest — contradicting (C2).

Pick such a vertex $ u $ on $ W_0 $.

**Step 3 — Splice in a sub-circuit at $ u $.** Apply Step $ 1 $ inside $ G' $, starting at $ u $. This produces a closed walk $ W_1 $ from $ u $ back to $ u $, using only edges of $ G' $ (i.e., not in $ W_0 $).

Splice $ W_1 $ into $ W_0 $ at the vertex $ u $: when $ W_0 $ reaches $ u $, detour through $ W_1 $ (returning to $ u $), then continue $ W_0 $. The resulting closed walk uses every edge of $ W_0 \cup W_1 $ exactly once — i.e., *strictly more* edges than $ W_0 $.

**Step 4 — Iterate.** Replace $ W_0 $ by the spliced walk and return to Step $ 2 $. Each iteration uses strictly more edges; since $ |E| $ is finite, the process terminates with all edges of $ G $ used. The final walk is an Eulerian circuit. $ \blacksquare $

## Examples

!(90%)[A 5-vertex Eulerian bowtie and the non-Eulerian K_4](problem-6-examples.svg "Left: the 'bowtie' graph (two triangles sharing a vertex) has degree sequence (2, 2, 4, 2, 2) — all even — so it has an Eulerian circuit. Right: K_4 has degree sequence (3, 3, 3, 3) — all odd, four of them — so it has neither an Eulerian circuit nor an Eulerian path.")

- **Bowtie (Eulerian).** Two triangles sharing vertex $ C $. Degrees are $ A = 2,\, B = 2,\, C = 4,\, D = 2,\, E = 2 $ — all even, graph connected. An explicit Eulerian circuit:

  $$$
  A \to B \to C \to D \to E \to C \to A.
  $$$

  Six edges, each used once, returning to the start. ✓

- **$ K_4 $ (not Eulerian).** Every vertex has degree $ 3 $; there are four odd-degree vertices, so (C1) fails. By the theorem, no Eulerian circuit. (And no Eulerian path either — that would need exactly two odd-degree vertices.)

## Eulerian Path (Companion Result)

An *Eulerian path* is an *open* walk (distinct endpoints) traversing every edge once. The companion theorem:

> *A connected graph $ G $ has an Eulerian path from $ u $ to $ v $ (with $ u \ne v $) iff exactly $ \{u, v\} $ are the odd-degree vertices.*

*Proof sketch.* Necessity is as before: interior vertices of the path are visited in pairs (enter, leave); the two endpoints contribute one extra unpaired incidence each. Sufficiency reduces to the circuit case: add a phantom edge $ uv $ to $ G $, making $ u $ and $ v $ even-degree; the result has all even degrees and is connected, so by Euler's theorem it has an Eulerian circuit. Deleting the phantom edge from the circuit gives an Eulerian path from $ u $ to $ v $ in $ G $.

This is exactly the criterion applied to the four graphs of Problem $ 1 $ of this section: count the odd-degree vertices — $ 0 $ for a circuit, $ 2 $ for a path, anything else means neither.

## Remarks

- **Königsberg.** Euler's original $ 1736 $ paper introduced the modern graph-theoretic formulation specifically to solve the Königsberg bridge problem. That graph has $ 4 $ vertices (banks/islands) with degrees $ 3, 3, 3, 5 $ — all odd. By necessity, no Eulerian circuit and no Eulerian path: it is impossible to walk the city crossing every bridge once.

- **Hierholzer's algorithm.** The constructive proof above is by Carl Hierholzer ($ 1873 $). Implemented with appropriate data structures (a doubly-linked walk supporting $ O(1) $ splicing, and a queue of "splice candidate" vertices), it runs in $ O(|E|) $ time — linear in the edge count.

- **Directed graphs.** A digraph admits an Eulerian directed circuit iff its underlying undirected graph is connected *and* every vertex has $ \deg^+(v) = \deg^-(v) $ (in-degree equals out-degree). The proof is the directed analog of the above.

- **Chinese Postman Problem.** When (C1) fails, one cannot traverse every edge exactly once and return to start. The *Chinese postman* (or *route inspection*) problem asks for the shortest closed walk that traverses every edge *at least* once. It is solved by a clever reduction to minimum-weight matching of the odd-degree vertices — itself solvable in polynomial time — turning a parity obstruction into an algorithmic question.

- **Connection to Problem $ 2 $.** Necessity of (C1) sits on the same parity reasoning as Problem $ 2 $'s "every graph has an even number of odd nodes." Both are corollaries of the basic identity $ \sum_v \deg(v) = 2|E| $.
